<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick III of Nuremberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1220–<a href="/years/1297">1297</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> was a Hohenzollern burgrave of Nuremberg who helped secure the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> as King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1273. In return Rudolf confirmed his family's hereditary rights. Frederick enlarged the Hohenzollern lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, including the inheritance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, laying the foundation of the later margraviates of Ansbach and Bayreuth.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was the eldest son of Conrad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and Adelheid of Frontenhausen, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>. He held the Hohenzollern possessions west of Nuremberg around the castle of Cadolzburg. In 1248 he received the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Counts of Andechs</a> through the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Meran</a> inheritance, which led to a quarrel with other noble houses that also claimed those lands. His first wife was Elisabeth of Merania, daughter and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>, Duke of Merania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On Conrad I's death in 1261 Frederick became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgrave of Nuremberg</a>. His attempt to push back Frankish power in the Main region met violent opposition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>. In 1273 he cast the deciding vote for his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> in the election of the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and the new king rewarded him by confirming his position as burgrave and entrusting him with the royal district court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>.</p><p>Frederick took part in the imperial war against the outlawed Otakar II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen</a> in 1278. He also had a territorial quarrel with Bohemia over the Egerland. In this period Wunsiedel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlangen">Erlangen</a> and Arzberg came into the possession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick's two sons by his first marriage, John and Siegmund, were murdered near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> around 1262, and his daughters married into the families of Öttingen, Castell, Schlüsselberg and Hohenlohe. On 10 April 1280 he married Helene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, daughter of Albert I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>. Their children included John I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgrave of Nuremberg</a>, and Frederick IV of Nuremberg, who continued the line. Frederick died on 14 August 1297.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick III of Nuremberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Frederick III of Nuremberg</b> helped elect Rudolf I in 1273.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgraviate of Nuremberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Cadolzburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.45,11.08&amp;year=1270" aria-label="Nuremberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Frederick III of Nuremberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5612 5845h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5612 5845h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.45,11.08&amp;year=1270">What was here in 1270? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
